1.1.0 (2026-11-20)
- In ITEMS > MISC the Pip-Boy's own holotapes (Atomic Command, Reign of Grelok, Zeta Invaders) are listed A to Z after the case. They came out Z to A: the Pip-Boy's sort does not keep things in the order it was given when they rank the same, so equal ranks are now sorted by file name.
- Zeta Invaders (an official Pip-Boy app since firmware 1.2.0) is treated like the Pip-Boy's own Atomic Command and Reign of Grelok: it is no longer listed in the case as a custom holotape, it can never be hidden from ITEMS > MISC (even if it was hidden before), and MISC lists it near the top with them. It is recognised by its file name in APPINFO starting with ZETA.
- Pip-Boy firmware 1.2.0: ITEMS > MISC now sorts the holotapes A to Z, so the order you set in the case (and the case itself at the top) was lost there; hidden tapes stayed hidden. The MISC hook now keeps the case's order on 1.2.0 as well as on older firmware. Checked in a desktop copy of the Pip-Boy's engine with the Pip-Boy's own MISC screen from both 1.1.x and 1.2.0. The hook only reorders and hides holotapes from APPINFO; the Pip-Boy's own MISC items (such as Zeta Invaders) are left alone.
- After MAKE FAVOURITE, HIDE FROM MISC or another option, the list stays where it was. Before, it scrolled so the tape you had just changed became the top row, hiding the tapes above it (found in a full scripted playthrough with the Pip-Boy's own list code: favourites, hiding, moving, every sort and HIDE OR SHOW ALL choice, PLAY HOLOTAPE, a missing OPTIONS.JS). If the tape has moved out of view (after a sort), the list still jumps to it.
- The startup loader that brings this holotape's hook back after a restart (loader v4) runs each hook with new Function instead of eval. With eval, every hook that stays switched on (the STATS rows, the MISC hand-off, the journal in DATA) kept the loader's working space, and with it the hook's whole source text, in memory for as long as the Pip-Boy was on. Measured in a desktop copy of the Pip-Boy's own JavaScript engine (Espruino 2v29): all our hooks together held about 970 blocks after a restart; now about 170. Nothing in the Pip-Boy's own firmware or menus changes. The first restart after updating still uses the old loader once and replaces it.
- The startup loader that keeps the MISC hook working after a restart is quieter and more careful (after Aidan's Lab's review of the firmware-update problem): it never prints or logs anything to the USB console, so it can no longer upset a firmware update or the companion app; it waits for the Pip-Boy's own code (Pip, the screen, the list maker and the settings reader) to finish starting before running any hook, and if that never happens it quietly gives up after a minute instead; it runs only once per start; and errors are kept in RM_HOLO_ERR for the terminal instead.
- Opening the tape upgrades the old loader in place. The loader carries a version, so newer and older hooks no longer take turns rewriting it, and hooks from before v2 are skipped at startup until their tape is updated.
- The hook frees its setup code once it has run, so it holds less memory while the Pip-Boy is on.
- The MISC hook now keeps only a small file check in memory. The list filter and the full hand-off are loaded from the card only while ITEMS > MISC builds its list, and are switched off again straight after, so every other screen and holotape has more memory (the old hook held about 150 blocks all the time).
- The first time the case opens (MISC hook not running yet), the hook starts just after the case has opened instead of during startup, once the Pip-Boy has freed the ITEMS > MISC list, so it cannot run out of memory there. Same pattern as the games (Cody's #130).

1.0.2 (2026-11-06)
- Fixed the MISC hook not starting on Pip-Boys whose internal storage is full: writing its startup slot failed with "Unable to find or create file" and stopped the hook, so the case showed HOOK NOT RUNNING and nothing was hidden. The hook now switches on anyway; if the startup slot can't be written, opening the case once after a full restart switches it on again.
- A full or write-locked SD card no longer crashes the case: favourites, hidden tapes or an order that cannot be written are skipped.
- If OPTIONS.JS cannot be loaded, the case returns to the list with a notice instead of a blank screen.

1.0.1 (2026-10-26)
- Fixed hiding holotapes from ITEMS > MISC on SD cards with no USER folder: the MISC hook stopped with NO_PATH before it switched on, so hidden tapes still showed in MISC and the case said HOOK FILE MISSING. The hook now creates the USER folder, and still switches on even if it cannot register for startup.
- When the hook is on the card but not running, the case now says HOOK NOT RUNNING instead of HOOK FILE MISSING.

1.0.0 (2026-10-18)
https://github.com/CodyTolene/pip-boy-3000-holotapes/pull/124
- Initial release.
- Starting a tape from ITEMS > MISC is now a full hand-off too: MISC is cleared from memory before the tape loads. With many tapes shown in MISC, bigger tapes like Lockpick could run out of memory when started from there.
- PLAY HOLOTAPE does a full hand-off: the tape starts once the case has fully closed, so the case no longer takes up memory the tape needs (fixes low memory when opening bigger tapes like My Journal from the case).
- Fixes a low-memory error: the hook is skipped when it is already running and listed for startup, and otherwise runs at the top level (new Function) so it keeps nothing of the app in memory after the app closes.
- No CFW file or boot loader needed: the MISC hook now ships with the holotape as HOLO/HOLOTAPE_CASE/MISC_HOOK.JS. It switches on when you open the case and registers itself in one shared boot slot (.boot1-.boot3, never .boot0) so it keeps working after a full restart.
- The hook does nothing if it is already active.
- Uses less memory while you browse: the options menus, sorting and Move in list are now a second file, OPTIONS.JS, loaded only while the options are open. The main file is about 40% smaller.
- Dropped the one-time move of tapes back from 1.0's STORED folder (any 1.1 or later has already done it).
- Organise your case: Move in list carries a tape up or down as you turn the left wheel; press to put it down.
- Sort the case A to Z, favourites to the top, or hidden to the bottom.
- ITEMS > MISC uses the same order (needs the updated CFW/52_RADIOMARU_MISC.JS and a reboot), with Holotape Case first.
- Making a tape a favourite no longer moves it; use Favourites to the top.
- Atomic Command and Grelok count as the Pip-Boy's own holotapes: the case leaves them out, Hide all never hides them, and ITEMS > MISC lists them first after the case.
- Holotapes whose icon is missing from the card show the Pip-Boy's own holotape picture (APPINFO/HOLO.IMG) instead of a drawn cassette.
- Options are grouped: Sort the case and Hide or show all open their own menus.
- Holotapes now only play from the new Play holotape option. Pressing or holding the left wheel opens the options, so no stray press can start a game.
- New option: Hide all custom holotapes. Hides every holotape installed from pip-boy.com, leaving only the Pip-Boy's own MISC items and the case.
- Press the left wheel for the options and hold it to play, so a stray press no longer starts a holotape.
- Now a submenu of ITEMS > MISC built on the Pip-Boy's own scrolling list, with the ITEMS header and tabs, instead of a separate full-screen app.
- Uses far less memory: only the .info file names are kept, and each tape's name and icon are read as its row comes on screen.
- Favourites show the filled box the Pip-Boy uses for equipped items. Hold the left wheel for the options.
- Hides holotapes from ITEMS > MISC with a CFW patch instead of moving .info files, so pip-boy.com still sees every holotape.
- Changes show the next time ITEMS > MISC opens, without a reboot.
- "Store all others" is now "Hide all but favourites".
- Tapes stored by 1.0 are moved back into APPINFO and kept hidden.
